WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.430
Last year our department bought

2
00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.170
forty development kits and set a

3
00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.010
capstone in week one.

4
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:11.110
By week six, half the teams were still

5
00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.190
debugging their first blinking light.

6
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:17.660
Today: how to sequence hardware labs into

7
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.520
a capstone, keep them safe and affordable,

8
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.400
and assess teams fairly with a rubric.

9
00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.890
Scaffolding means structured support

10
00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:29.150
that is removed step by step.

11
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.840
Early labs give working code

12
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.360
and one change to make.

13
00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.840
Later labs give a goal and a parts list.

14
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.840
The capstone gives only a problem.

15
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.240
One proven sequence: digital input and

16
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.280
output, then sensors and the

17
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.020
converter, then timers and interrupts,

18
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.980
then low power, then networking.

19
00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.660
Each lab produces a part the

20
00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.000
capstone will reuse.

21
00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.290
Here is a fourteen-week split that

22
00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.190
worked: eight weeks of scaffolded labs,

23
00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.270
a week to propose, and five weeks of

24
00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.190
capstone with a midpoint demo.

25
00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:10.470
Keep labs at low voltage, and keep lithium

26
00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.390
cells charged only on proper chargers.

27
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.720
Simulators let students test code before

28
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.760
touching hardware, and shared kits with a

29
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.500
checkout log keep costs down.

30
00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.550
A working demo can hide luck.

31
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.860
Assess the engineering too: a requirements

32
00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.240
list, a measured power budget, a test log,

33
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.100
and an individual reflection.

34
00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:35.490
Peer assessment helps spread

35
00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.450
marks fairly inside a team.

36
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.080
Quick check!

37
00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.890
Why did half our teams stall

38
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:44.290
in the first six weeks?

39
00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.850
They faced an open problem before

40
00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.070
they had the basic skills.

41
00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:50.790
The kits were fine.

42
00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:54.010
A scaffolded sequence of labs would

43
00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.450
have built those skills first.

44
00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.710
In the app, open the board lab.

45
00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:01.730
Run “Night light” on a virtual board,

46
00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.470
change its code, and run it again before

47
00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.390
anyone touches real hardware.

48
00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:08.190
Your turn.

49
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.880
In a team of four, one

50
00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.000
student did almost nothing.

51
00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.410
Which evidence best supports

52
00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:17.110
adjusting individual marks?

53
00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.380
Structured peer assessment with

54
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:21.800
the commit and test logs.

55
00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:25.170
Remember: scaffold before the capstone,

56
00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.250
keep labs safe and cheap, and assess the

57
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.010
engineering as well as the demo.

58
00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.270
Try it this term: list the

59
00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:35.730
skills your capstone needs.

60
00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:38.510
Then check that an earlier lab

61
00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.630
teaches each one, and add a lab for

62
00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:42.810
any skill that is missing.
